+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Retatrutide Dosing: What the Trials Actually Used | Supplement Intelligence</title><meta name="description" content="Retatrutide is investigational and not FDA-approved. A plain-language review of the doses and schedules used in published Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials, reported results, side effects, and how it compares with tirzepatide. Not medical advice."><link rel="canonical" href="https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "Article", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide#article", "headline": "Retatrutide Dosing: What the Trials Actually Used", "alternativeHeadline": "Retatrutide: what the trials actually used", "description": "Retatrutide is investigational and not FDA-approved. A plain-language review of the doses and schedules used in published Phase 2 and Phase 3 trials, reported results, side effects, and how it compares with tirzepatide. Not medical advice.", "datePublished": "2026-11-10", "dateModified": "2026-11-10", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide", "mainEntityOfPage": {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide"}, "inLanguage": "en-US", "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Supplement Intelligence", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/"}, "about": ["retatrutide", "tirzepatide", "GLP-1", "obesity"]}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide#faq", "url": "https://supplement-intelligence.com/glp-1/retatrutide", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Is retatrutide FDA-approved?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. As of November 10, 2026, retatrutide is not an FDA-approved drug, and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration states that retatrutide and cagrilintide “cannot be used in compounding under federal law” and “have not been found safe and effective for any condition” (https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss)."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do retatrutide and tirzepatide compare in trials?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "In TRIUMPH-1, treatment-regimen (intention-to-treat) mean weight change at 80 weeks was −17.6%, −23.7%, and −25.0% with retatrutide 4, 9, and 12 mg, versus −3.9% with placebo. In SURMOUNT-1, the same estimand at 72 weeks was −15.0%, −19.5%, and −20.9% with tirzepatide 5, 10, and 15 mg, versus −3.1% with placebo. No head-to-head trial has been published. [PMIDs 42814954, 35658024]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What retatrutide doses did the clinical trials use?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The Phase 2 obesity trial studied weekly maintenance doses of 1, 4, 8, and 12 mg. The Phase 2 diabetes trial studied 0.5, 4, 8, and 12 mg. TRIUMPH-1 and TRIUMPH-2 studied 4, 9, and 12 mg; TRIUMPH-3 and TRIUMPH-4 studied 9 and 12 mg. These are trial assignments, not instructions for use. [PMIDs 37366315, 37385280, 41090431]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How much weight loss did retatrutide show in trials?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "In TRIUMPH-1, adults without diabetes assigned to 4, 9, or 12 mg had mean weight changes of −17.6%, −23.7%, and −25.0%, versus −3.9% with placebo at 80 weeks, using the treatment-regimen analysis. These are Grade A results within that trial, not predictions for an individual. [PMID 42814954]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What side effects were reported with retatrutide?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Gastrointestinal problems were the most common adverse events. TRIUMPH-2 also reported hypotension and dysesthesia, or altered skin sensation. Phase 2 reported heart-rate increases. The dedicated knee osteoarthritis paper reported more serious adverse events with retatrutide than placebo in both trials it analyzed. [PMIDs 37366315, 42810372, 43001755]"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can retatrutide be compounded or bought online?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Retatrutide cannot be compounded under federal law. FDA has warned about illegal sales of unapproved products containing retatrutide, including products falsely labeled for research purposes or not for human consumption. Those labels do not establish safety or quality. [FDA; reference 9]"}}]}]}</script></head><body><p><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/glp-1">GLP-1</a> / Retatrutide: what the trials actually used</p><h1>Retatrutide: what the trials actually used</h1><p>Evidence reviewed: November 10, 2026.</p><div class="si-verdict"><p><strong>Retatrutide is investigational, is not FDA-approved, and has not been found safe and effective for any condition. It cannot be compounded under federal law. This page is not medical advice.</strong> <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss">FDA</a></p><p>The published trials report weight loss and improvements in some other measured outcomes. That evidence does not establish safe use outside trials or make products sold online reliable.</p></div>
+<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Is retatrutide FDA-approved?</strong><br>No. As of November 10, 2026, retatrutide is not an FDA-approved drug, and the U.S. Food and Drug Administration states that retatrutide and cagrilintide “cannot be used in compounding under federal law” and “have not been found safe and effective for any condition” (https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss).</p>
+<p><strong>How do retatrutide and tirzepatide compare in trials?</strong><br>In TRIUMPH-1, treatment-regimen (intention-to-treat) mean weight change at 80 weeks was −17.6%, −23.7%, and −25.0% with retatrutide 4, 9, and 12 mg, versus −3.9% with placebo. In SURMOUNT-1, the same estimand at 72 weeks was −15.0%, −19.5%, and −20.9% with tirzepatide 5, 10, and 15 mg, versus −3.1% with placebo. No head-to-head trial has been published. [PMIDs 42814954, 35658024]</p>
+<p><strong>What retatrutide doses did the clinical trials use?</strong><br>The Phase 2 obesity trial studied weekly maintenance doses of 1, 4, 8, and 12 mg. The Phase 2 diabetes trial studied 0.5, 4, 8, and 12 mg. TRIUMPH-1 and TRIUMPH-2 studied 4, 9, and 12 mg; TRIUMPH-3 and TRIUMPH-4 studied 9 and 12 mg. These are trial assignments, not instructions for use. [PMIDs 37366315, 37385280, 41090431]</p>
+<p><strong>How much weight loss did retatrutide show in trials?</strong><br>In TRIUMPH-1, adults without diabetes assigned to 4, 9, or 12 mg had mean weight changes of −17.6%, −23.7%, and −25.0%, versus −3.9% with placebo at 80 weeks, using the treatment-regimen analysis. These are Grade A results within that trial, not predictions for an individual. [PMID 42814954]</p>
+<p><strong>What side effects were reported with retatrutide?</strong><br>Gastrointestinal problems were the most common adverse events. TRIUMPH-2 also reported hypotension and dysesthesia, or altered skin sensation. Phase 2 reported heart-rate increases. The dedicated knee osteoarthritis paper reported more serious adverse events with retatrutide than placebo in both trials it analyzed. [PMIDs 37366315, 42810372, 43001755]</p>
+<p><strong>Can retatrutide be compounded or bought online?</strong><br>Retatrutide cannot be compounded under federal law. FDA has warned about illegal sales of unapproved products containing retatrutide, including products falsely labeled for research purposes or not for human consumption. Those labels do not establish safety or quality. [FDA; reference 9]</p>
+<h2>How to read the evidence</h2><p>Our evidence posture is cautious: distinguish published results from study plans, and keep each finding tied to the people and outcomes actually studied.</p><ul><li><strong>Grade A:</strong> a published Phase 3 randomized trial, for outcomes reported within that trial.</li><li><strong>Grade B:</strong> a published Phase 2 randomized trial; clearly labeled exploratory, nested, or subgroup analyses; design-only papers; registry results absent from the journal abstract; or clearly labeled company statements.</li><li><strong>Grade C:</strong> registered trials without published outcomes, predictions based on how a drug works, and comparisons between separate trials that did not match participants and methods.</li></ul>
+<h2>What retatrutide is</h2><p>Retatrutide, also called LY3437943, is a single peptide studied as a weekly injection under the skin. It activates three receptors: GIP, GLP-1, and glucagon. Tirzepatide activates GIP and GLP-1 receptors. This is established pharmacology, not a claim that the extra receptor makes retatrutide better. [PMIDs 37366315, 35658024]</p>
+<h2>What the evidence supports</h2><ul><li><strong>A:</strong> TRIUMPH-1 and TRIUMPH-2 reported greater average weight loss than placebo in adults without and with type 2 diabetes. [PMIDs 42814954, 42810372]</li><li><strong>A:</strong> Phase 3 studies reported improvements in knee osteoarthritis pain and sleep apnea breathing events. [PMIDs 43001755, 42904878]</li><li><strong>B:</strong> Phase 2 studies reported weight loss and, in type 2 diabetes, lower HbA1c. [PMIDs 37366315, 37385280]</li><li><strong>C:</strong> superiority to tirzepatide is not established. TRIUMPH-5 has no published results.</li></ul>
+<h2>What the trials actually used</h2><p>These are descriptions of research protocols, not instructions for taking retatrutide.</p><h3>Phase 2 obesity</h3><p>The trial enrolled 338 adults for 48 weeks. Some groups increased doses up to week 12.</p><div class="si-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Assigned maintenance dose</th><th>Starting dose and sequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 mg</td><td>1 mg; no escalation described</td></tr><tr><td>4 mg</td><td>2 mg followed by 4 mg, or 4 mg without a lower start</td></tr><tr><td>8 mg</td><td>2, 4, then 8 mg; or 4 followed by 8 mg</td></tr><tr><td>12 mg</td><td>2, 4, 8, then 12 mg</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Phase 2 type 2 diabetes</h3><p>The trial used weekly maintenance doses of 0.5, 4, 8, or 12 mg, placebo, or dulaglutide 1.5 mg. The study labels do not supply a complete timing schedule. <strong>Grade B.</strong></p>
+<h3>Phase 3 TRIUMPH</h3><div class="si-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Setting</th><th>Weekly doses</th><th>Duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TRIUMPH-1</td><td>Without diabetes; nested sleep apnea and knee arthritis groups</td><td>4, 9, or 12 mg vs placebo</td><td>80 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>TRIUMPH-2</td><td>With type 2 diabetes</td><td>4, 9, or 12 mg vs placebo</td><td>80 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>TRIUMPH-3</td><td>Established cardiovascular disease</td><td>9 or 12 mg vs placebo</td><td>80 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>TRIUMPH-4</td><td>Knee osteoarthritis</td><td>9 or 12 mg vs placebo</td><td>68 weeks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+<h2>Phase 2 results</h2><p>At 48 weeks, weight changes were −8.7% with 1 mg, −17.1% with combined 4 mg, −22.8% with combined 8 mg, −24.2% with 12 mg, and −2.1% with placebo. Combined groups pool different starting doses. <strong>Grade B.</strong> [PMID 37366315]</p><p>In the diabetes trial, HbA1c reductions at 24 weeks ranged from −0.43% to −2.02%; 36-week weight decreases ranged from 3.19% to 16.94% across retatrutide groups. These are Phase 2 findings, not TRIUMPH-2. [PMID 37385280]</p>
+<h2>Phase 3 results</h2><h3>Weight: TRIUMPH-1 and TRIUMPH-2</h3><div class="si-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Without diabetes</th><th>With type 2 diabetes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4 mg</td><td>−17.6%</td><td>−11.9%</td></tr><tr><td>9 mg</td><td>−23.7%</td><td>−16.8%</td></tr><tr><td>12 mg</td><td>−25.0%</td><td>−18.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Placebo</td><td>−3.9%</td><td>−5.1%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are treatment-regimen results at 80 weeks. <strong>Grade A within each trial.</strong> The columns describe different studies and populations.</p><h3>Knee osteoarthritis pain</h3><p>TRIUMPH-4 enrolled 445 participants. At 68 weeks, WOMAC pain changes were −3.96 with 9 mg and −3.74 with 12 mg, versus −2.12 with placebo. Weight changes were −20.0%, −23.7%, and −4.6%. <strong>Grade A.</strong> [PMID 43001755]</p><h3>Obstructive sleep apnea</h3><p>In nested groups, differences from placebo in breathing interruptions ranged from −14.4 to −24.4 events per hour, depending on population and dose. Both hybrid and ITT analyses support within-trial improvement. <strong>Grade A.</strong> [PMID 42904878]</p>
+<h2>Side effects and safety context</h2><p>Stomach and bowel problems were the most common adverse events. Phase 2 heart-rate increases peaked at 24 weeks and declined. In TRIUMPH-2, diarrhea occurred in 27%, 34%, and 34% of the 4, 9, and 12 mg groups versus 13% with placebo; nausea in 14%, 21%, and 28% versus 8%. Hypotension and dysesthesia were also reported. Trial findings do not establish long-term safety outside research.</p>
+<h2>Retatrutide versus tirzepatide</h2><p>No published head-to-head trial was identified as of November 10, 2026. TRIUMPH-5 is registered without results. Separate trial figures do not establish superiority. TRIUMPH-1 reported −17.6% to −25.0% at 80 weeks; SURMOUNT-1 reported −15.0% to −20.9% at 72 weeks. The trials differed in duration, dose families, protocols, and counselling.</p>
+<h2>What is still unknown</h2><p>Long-term safety outside trials, cardiovascular outcomes, routine-care use, and TRIUMPH-3 and TRIUMPH-5 outcomes are not established. A Lilly page states a planned 2027 application; that is a company plan, not approval. FDA warns about illegal online sales and unknown quality.</p>
+<h2>Primary sources</h2><ol class="si-sources"><li>Jastreboff AM et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37366315/">Phase 2 obesity trial</a>. PMID 37366315.</li><li>Rosenstock J et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37385280/">Phase 2 diabetes trial</a>. PMID 37385280.</li><li>Jastreboff AM et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42814954/">TRIUMPH-1</a>. PMID 42814954.</li><li>Bellido V et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42810372/">TRIUMPH-2</a>. PMID 42810372.</li><li>Giblin K et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41090431/">TRIUMPH design</a>. PMID 41090431.</li><li>Schnitzer TJ et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43001755/">Knee osteoarthritis</a>.</li><li>Grunstein RR et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42904878/">Sleep apnea</a>.</li><li>Jastreboff AM et al. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35658024/">SURMOUNT-1</a>.</li><li><a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-alerts-and-statements/fdas-concerns-unapproved-glp-1-drugs-used-weight-loss">FDA concerns with unapproved GLP-1 drugs</a>.</li></ol><h2>Related evidence</h2><ul><li><a href="/glp-1">GLP-1 evidence</a></li><li><a href="/glp-1/muscle-loss">GLP-1 use and muscle loss</a></li><li><a href="/ingredients/creatine-monohydrate">Creatine monohydrate evidence</a></li><li><a href="/ingredients/magnesium">Magnesium evidence</a></li><li><a href="/references">How we handle references</a></li></ul><p><strong>Important:</strong> This page summarizes published trials. It is not medical advice, a recommendation to start or stop a medicine, or an instruction to use retatrutide.</p></body></html>
